<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Our Classroom</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What's in Your Bag?</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Describe the purpose of everyday texts. Reading: Outline the meaning of simple paragraphs. Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can discover and underline or circle important words in paragraphs about classroom items (e.g., circling the word "bag" in the sentence "My bag is red."). Can read and outline main points in paragraphs about classroom objects by responding to questions (e.g., "What's on the desk?" "A notebook."). Can blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable and two-syllable words such as prepositions or objects (e.g., blending /ɔ-n/ to form the word "on" or /b-æ-g/ to form the word "bag").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary and pronunciation for the reading task.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows their school bag and takes out some classroom items (e.g., a <b>book</b>, a <b>pencil</b>, an <b>eraser</b>, a <b>notebook</b>). The teacher asks, "What's this?" for each item, eliciting quick responses from students. The teacher reviews prepositions by placing items in, on, or next to the bag. "The book is <b>in</b> my bag."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of /b/ in "<b>book</b>" and /t/ in "<b>table</b>" by exaggerating the sounds. The teacher then models blending phonemes for familiar words: "Listen: /ɔ-n/. What word is it? <b>On</b>." "Listen: /b-æ-g/. What word is it? <b>Bag</b>." Students repeat chorally.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up a book) "Is this a marker?" (Expected answer: "No, it's a book.")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher places a pencil on a desk) "Is the pencil <b>in</b> the desk?" (Expected answer: "No, it's <b>on</b> the desk.")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content through a short, simple reading text related to the scenario "Discovering Our Classroom" and theme "What's in Your Bag?".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short descriptive paragraph on a large chart paper or projected slide, titled "My Classroom and My Bag". The text uses prepositions of place, present simple, WH-questions (implied context), and key vocabulary (<b>desk, book, chair, notebook, pencil case, bag, ruler, crayon, marker, board</b>).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text: "Welcome to my classroom! The <b>board</b> is large and white. There are many <b>desks</b> and <b>chairs</b>. My <b>desk</b> is <b>next to</b> the window. My <b>bag</b> is <b>on</b> my <b>chair</b>. <b>What's in</b> my <b>bag</b>? My <b>notebook</b>, a blue <b>pen</b>, and my red <b>pencil case</b>. My <b>ruler</b> is <b>in</b> my <b>pencil case</b>. A green <b>crayon</b> and a black <b>marker</b> are <b>on</b> my <b>desk</b>. Everything is neat!"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Outline the meaning of simple paragraphs about familiar objects and their locations in the classroom.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher reads the text aloud, pointing to words. Students listen and follow. Then, the teacher asks, "Is this text about food or school?" (Expected: "School.")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads simple statements based on the text. Students show thumbs up for True, thumbs down for False. E.g., "The board is small." (False). "My bag is on my chair." (True).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> The teacher asks students to circle or underline all the objects they can find in the bag. (e.g., <b>notebook, pen, pencil case, ruler</b>).</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Activities focused on accuracy in reading, using target grammar, vocabulary, and phonemic awareness, directly from the presented text.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading simple paragraphs, identifying key information using WH-questions, prepositions of place (<b>in, on, next to</b>), present simple, and classroom vocabulary (<b>desk, book, marker, board, chair, notebook, ruler, pencil case, pen, crayon, bag</b>). Phonemic awareness for one and two-syllable words like prepositions and objects.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Read and Circle):</b> Students receive a worksheet with the "My Classroom and My Bag" text. They read the text again silently. The teacher instructs them to <b>circle</b> all the prepositions of place (<b>in, on, next to</b>) and <b>underline</b> all the classroom objects.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Complete the Sentence):</b> Students read incomplete sentences based on the text and choose the correct word from a word bank or multiple-choice options.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "My bag is ____ my chair." (a. in / b. on / c. next to)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "My ____ is in my pencil case." (a. crayon / b. ruler / c. marker)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Phonemic Blending Check (Read and Blend):</b> The teacher displays flashcards with single phonemes or simple consonant-vowel combinations (e.g., /b/, /æ/, /g/). Students are guided to blend the sounds to form familiar words from the text (e.g., blending /b-æ-g/ to form "bag," /p-eɪp-ər/ to form "paper," /t-eɪ-bəl/ to form "table," /ɔ-n/ to form "on"). Students read the blended word aloud.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Pair Interaction (Read and Answer):</b> In pairs, students take turns reading a question about the text (e.g., "<b>What's in</b> the <b>pencil case</b>?") and answering it based on the text they have in front of them (e.g., "A <b>ruler</b> is <b>in</b> the <b>pencil case</b>."). They check each other's answers. This fosters respectful classroom interaction ("Excuse me, can you read that again?").</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use the language actively in a realistic reading scenario, focusing on fluency and comprehension for "Discovering Our Classroom."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Read and Draw My Desk"</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a new, slightly different paragraph describing items on or around a desk or in a bag, with some new adjectives (e.g., "My <b>small</b> <b>pencil case</b> is <b>on</b> my <b>neat desk</b>. A <b>colorful</b> <b>crayon</b> is <b>next to</b> my <b>notebook</b>. My <b>light blue</b> <b>eraser</b> is <b>in</b> my <b>pencil case</b>.").</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student reads their unique short description.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Based on what they read, students draw the items in their correct locations on a simple outline drawing of a desk provided on a worksheet.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on accurate comprehension of the descriptive text and prepositions to correctly illustrate the scene.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher observes students as they read silently and then draw, providing assistance with vocabulary or text interpretation as needed.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to check students' ability to describe and outline meaning from simple paragraphs and blend phonemes.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Read and Respond"</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a short, new paragraph (3-4 sentences) about a different classroom area or bag.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the paragraph and then answer 2-3 simple WH-questions (e.g., "<b>What</b> is <b>on</b> the <b>shelf</b>?" "<b>Where</b> is <b>her map</b>?") by writing one-word or short sentence answers.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Additionally, students will be asked to verbally blend two words with previously encountered phonemes (e.g., /k-r-æ-y-ə-n/ for "crayon," /m-a-r-k-ə-r/ for "marker") for individual assessment of phonemic awareness.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying key information from the text.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target vocabulary (nouns, prepositions).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to blend spoken phonemes to form one- or two-syllable words.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of written responses.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two statements:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I learned to read about _______________."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now find the word ______ in a sentence."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson's effectiveness and planning for future adjustments and progression within the unit sequence.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about the classroom was easy for me today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word or sentence was difficult to understand or read?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., visual support, pairing, explicit phonemic blending practice).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for future reading activities to better support student comprehension and engagement (e.g., more challenging texts, different question types, additional vocabulary review)?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to successfully blend phonemes for the target words? If not, what scaffolds are needed?</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will focus on using the learned vocabulary and grammar in spoken interactions. Students will ask and answer questions about classroom objects and their locations, building on the reading comprehension of this lesson. We will use the identified objects and locations from today's texts in oral dialogues.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>